eature a shield with three silver chevrons on a blue background, accompanied by a golden crown and a pair of crossed silver keys. This assignment was more than just a formal decision; it was a testament to the municipality's rich history and cultural heritage. The three silver chevrons, for instance, represented the three rivers that flowed through Noorbeek, symbolizing the region's connection to the natural world. The golden crown signified the municipality's sovereignty and autonomy, while the crossed silver keys represented the keys to the region's prosperity and growth. The assignment of the coat of arms was a momentous occasion, marking a new era of identity and unity for the people of Noorbeek. The coat of arms became an integral part of the municipality's daily life, adorning official documents, flags, and even the local architecture. It was a constant reminder of the region's history, culture, and values, serving as a beacon of pride for the community."}], "fallible_prior": [], "prior_explanation": "I am uncertain about this specific event. My knowledge does not contain records of the coat of arms of Noorbeek being involved in any significant historical event."}
